---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const REPO = 'https://github.com/JonasGrunau/open_audio_analyzer';
const DOCS = 'https://jonasgrunau.github.io/open_audio_analyzer';
const V = '0.8.0';

const ARTEFACTS = [
  {
    os: 'macOS',
    req: '14.2 Sonoma or later',
    file: `Open.Audio.Analyzer-${V}-macos.pkg`,
    plugin: 'VST3 + AU',
    note: 'Universal — Apple silicon and Intel. Installs the plugin behind a checkbox that starts ticked.',
  },
  {
    os: 'Windows',
    req: '10 build 1809 or later',
    file: `Open.Audio.Analyzer-${V}-windows-x64.exe`,
    plugin: 'VST3',
    note: 'Uninstaller appears in Installed apps.',
  },
  {
    os: 'Linux',
    req: 'x64 or arm64',
    file: `Open.Audio.Analyzer-${V}-linux-x64.tar.gz`,
    plugin: 'VST3',
    note: 'Run ./install.sh. No root required.',
  },
  {
    os: 'Linux · AppImage',
    req: 'GTK from the host',
    file: `Open.Audio.Analyzer-${V}-x86_64.AppImage`,
    plugin: '—',
    note: 'One file, no root. An AppImage never installs anything, so it cannot carry the plugin.',
  },
  {
    os: 'Linux · flatpak',
    req: 'flatpak 1.12+',
    file: `Open.Audio.Analyzer-${V}-x86_64.flatpak`,
    plugin: '—',
    note: 'Sandboxed, carries its own runtime. A flatpak’s plugin would be built against the sandbox’s libraries while the DAW that must load it runs against the host’s.',
  },
  {
    os: 'CLI · any platform',
    req: 'no Flutter runtime',
    file: 'oaa-cli-<platform>.tar.gz',
    plugin: '—',
    note: 'bin/oaa beside the engine as a shared library. An archive rather than one file, for that reason.',
  },
];

const CAPTURE = [
  [
    'Windows',
    'Nothing to do.',
    'WASAPI loopback captures whatever is playing. No driver, no setup.',
    'ready',
  ],
  [
    'macOS',
    'Nothing to do.',
    '<strong>System Output</strong> is the first entry in the source menu, named after the output device it is metering. It is a Core Audio process tap: it reads what is being sent to your speakers without rerouting it, so you keep hearing your audio. The first time you choose it, macOS asks permission to record system audio — and a refusal is silent, so the meters read digital black rather than saying no. 14.2 is where the tapping API arrived and it is also the application’s minimum, so there is no supported version where the entry is missing.',
    'ready',
  ],
  [
    'Linux',
    'Nothing to do.',
    'A PulseAudio or PipeWire monitor source already appears in the list.',
    'ready',
  ],
];

const CONFIG = [
  ['macOS', '~/Library/Application Support/Open Audio Analyzer'],
  ['Windows', '%APPDATA%\\Open Audio Analyzer'],
  ['Linux', '$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/oaa, or ~/.config/oaa'],
  ['iPadOS', 'Library/Application Support/… inside the app’s own container'],
  ['Android', 'oaa inside the app’s own files directory'],
];
---

<Base
  title="Download Open Audio Analyzer 0.8.0"
  description="Installers for macOS, Windows and Linux, an AppImage, a flatpak and the oaa command-line analyser. Metering your own system's output needs no driver on any platform."
  path="/download"
>
  <section class="head shell">
    <p class="label">Install</p>
    <h1>Download<br />{V}</h1>
    <p class="prose head-p">
      Every release publishes five desktop downloads and the CLI on the releases page. The first
      three installers carry the VST3 and Audio Unit plugin and install it as well, behind a
      checkbox that starts ticked; untick it and you get the application on its own.
    </p>
    <div class="cta">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href={`${REPO}/releases/latest`} rel="noopener">
        Go to the releases page
      </a>
      <a class="btn" href={`${DOCS}/install.html`} rel="noopener">Full install guide</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section shell">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div>
        <p class="label">Artefacts</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">Six artefacts</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="readout">checksums on the release</p>
    </div>

    <ul class="arts">
      {
        ARTEFACTS.map((a) => (
          <li class="art module">
            <div class="module-bar">
              <span class="name name-brand">{a.os}</span>
              <span class="val">{a.req}</span>
            </div>
            <div class="art-body">
              <p class="art-file num">{a.file}</p>
              <p class="art-note">{a.note}</p>
              <p class="art-plug">
                <span class="label art-plabel">Plugin</span>
                <span class="num">{a.plugin}</span>
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <div class="notes">
      <div class="note">
        <h3>There is no Mac App Store build, and there will not be one.</h3>
        <p class="prose note-p">
          The store requires the app sandbox, and a sandboxed application has its home directory
          redirected into <code>~/Library/Containers</code> — which would put every preset, skin and
          delivery target somewhere no user goes looking, and somewhere no override could escape.
          Open Audio Analyzer is distributed directly instead. The pkg is signed with a Developer ID
          <em>Installer</em> certificate — a different one from the Developer ID Application
          certificate that signs the code — and notarised when a release is built with the
          credentials for both.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="note">
        <h3>The iPad build goes to TestFlight, not to the releases page.</h3>
        <p class="prose note-p">
          A tagged release builds it, signs it for the App Store and uploads it — after the release
          is published, so a TestFlight build always belongs to a release that exists. The IPA is
          not attached as an asset because an App Store signature provisions no devices: a file you
          downloaded could not be installed on your iPad, by you or by anyone. Building it yourself
          is two lines and needs no credentials at all.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="note">
        <h3>A build from a fork is unsigned, and says so.</h3>
        <p class="prose note-p">
          Signing needs certificates that are not ours to commit. Each packaging script produces an
          unsigned artefact and reports which of its three states it was in, rather than failing —
          a fork has no secrets, and a build that stopped there would be useless to it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section shell">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div>
        <p class="label">Capturing system output</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">What each platform<br />needs</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="readout">no driver on any platform</p>
    </div>

    <ul class="caps">
      {
        CAPTURE.map(([os, verdict, body, state]) => (
          <li class={`cap cap-${state}`}>
            <div class="cap-head">
              <h3 class="cap-os">{os}</h3>
              <span class="cap-verdict num">{verdict}</span>
            </div>
            <p class="prose cap-b" set:html={body} />
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <p class="prose caps-note">
      Metering a hardware input needs none of this — any interface shows up directly. Two rough
      edges on the macOS tap, stated rather than hidden: it follows the default output device when
      you change it, but only when the new device has the same sample rate and channel count,
      because the engine’s DSP is sized once and cannot be resized underneath a running
      measurement. And if macOS denies the permission, a tap delivers silence rather than an error —
      if the meters sit at the floor with something obviously playing, check Privacy &amp; Security.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section shell">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div>
        <p class="label">Configuration directory</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">Where settings, presets<br />and skins live</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="readout">plain JSON · OAA_CONFIG_DIR overrides</p>
    </div>

    <div class="cfg-split">
      <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Configuration directories">
      <table class="spec">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Platform</th>
            <th scope="col">Directory</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            CONFIG.map(([os, dir]) => (
              <tr>
                <td class="q">{os}</td>
                <td><code class="cfg-code">{dir}</code></td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
      </div>

      <div class="stack-24">
        <p class="prose">
          Settings, presets, your own delivery targets and your own skins are plain JSON files you
          can open, edit, copy between machines or keep in version control. The two tablet rows are
          the ones you cannot open in a file manager, because both systems give an app a private
          directory and no way out of it — Settings → Session prints the path.
        </p>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>OAA_CONFIG_DIR</code> overrides the three desktop rows, for a portable install or
          for keeping configuration alongside your dotfiles. <code>--config-dir=&lt;path&gt;</code> on
          the command line beats the variable in turn, which is the one that works on macOS, where
          an environment cannot be handed to an application bundle.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section shell">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div>
        <p class="label">Building from source</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">Two commands</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="readout">Flutter 3.44.5-stable · a C toolchain</p>
    </div>

    <div class="build module">
      <div class="module-bar">
        <span class="name">from source</span>
        <span class="val">Xcode CLT, MSVC, or gcc/clang</span>
      </div>
      <pre class="term-body"><code><span class="c">$</span> git clone https://github.com/JonasGrunau/open_audio_analyzer
<span class="c">$</span> cd open_audio_analyzer && flutter run -d macos</code></pre>
    </div>

    <p class="prose build-note">
      The Flutter version is pinned in <code>.tool-versions</code>. Full instructions for every
      platform, including the iPad build, are on the
      <a class="link" href={`${DOCS}/building.html`} rel="noopener">documentation site</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .head {
    padding-block: var(--s96) var(--s64);
  }

  .head h1 {
    margin-top: var(--s24);
  }

  .head-p {
    margin-top: var(--s32);
  }

  .cta {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--s12);
    margin-top: var(--s32);
  }

  .arts {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--s16);
  }

  /* Platform names keep the case their makers write them in. The silkscreen
     tracking still reads as a label without shouting "MACOS" at anybody. */
  .name-brand {
    text-transform: none;
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .art-body {
    padding: var(--s16);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--s12);
    flex: 1;
  }

  .art-file {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--signal);
    word-break: break-all;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .art-note {
    font-family: var(--prose);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--faint);
    flex: 1;
  }

  .art-plug {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--s12);
    padding-top: var(--s12);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hair);
  }

  .art-plabel {
    font-size: 0.5625rem;
  }

  .art-plug .num {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .notes {
    margin-top: var(--s64);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--s48);
  }

  .note {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hair-strong);
    padding-top: var(--s16);
  }

  .note h3 {
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-weight: 650;
    line-height: 1.3;
    max-width: 32ch;
  }

  .note-p {
    margin-top: var(--s12);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    max-width: none;
  }

  .caps {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .cap {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hair);
    padding: var(--s24) 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 320px) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--s32);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .cap:first-child {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hair-strong);
  }

  .cap-head {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--s8);
  }

  .cap-os {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    font-weight: 650;
  }

  .cap-verdict {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
  }

  .cap-ready .cap-verdict {
    color: var(--signal);
  }

  .cap-b {
    max-width: none;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }

  .caps-note {
    margin-top: var(--s32);
    padding-top: var(--s24);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hair);
    max-width: 78ch;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }

  .cfg-split {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--s48);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .cfg-code {
    white-space: normal;
    word-break: break-word;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
  }

  .term-body {
    margin: 0;
    padding: var(--s16);
    overflow-x: auto;
    font-family: var(--readout);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .term-body .c {
    color: var(--signal);
    user-select: none;
  }

  .build-note {
    margin-top: var(--s24);
  }

  @media (max-width: 1080px) {
    .arts,
    .notes {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
    .cfg-split {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: var(--s32);
    }
    .cap {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: var(--s12);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 680px) {
    .head {
      padding-block: var(--s64) var(--s32);
    }
    .arts,
    .notes {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: var(--s16);
    }
    .notes {
      gap: var(--s32);
    }
  }
</style>
